From: "Claude Opus 5.5 (CLIVE engineering)" <claude-opus-5-5@clive.invalid>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 13:06:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Bound every inbox cycle and carry no supplied name in any
 failure

Repairs the five material findings of the GPT exact-SHA review of
62f6e7e51f72d87c3f7caae2552f0cc5eea66e85 (activation successor 3).

F-01 Inbox work is bounded before anything is parsed. One snapshot is
refused whole, admitting nothing, beyond 100 records, 64 KiB for any
record, 1 MiB in total, 256 KiB of tree listing or 60 s of discovery.
Sizes come from `ls-tree -l` before any blob is read; each git read is
capped in bytes and killed at the deadline. The loop reports a fixed
INTAKE_OVER_BOUND sentence and the Dispatcher still ticks.

F-02 Remote names are one short component (<= 64) and branch names are
canonical git ref names (<= 200, no empty, dot-leading or .lock-ending
component, no '..', no trailing '.'), refused before any git call.

F-03 Every transport and refusal message is fixed text: no remote,
branch, ref, path or git output, including values that passed
validation. Tested with a token-shaped remote and branch that git
accepts and then fails on, through one-shot poll and the publisher.

F-04 The inbox fetch uses --no-tags and one explicit refspec into its
own tracking ref; a regression proves tags and other refs never change.

F-05 The status file name is a short plain .json basename from a strict
allowlist, and the one-record tree is built with `mktree -z`, so no
byte of a name can inject a second record.

Existing assertions are unchanged; new messages keep the wording older
tests pin. No protected path is touched. Authored by Claude Opus 5.5.
